Who is the most important person in the kingdom?
At that hour, the disciples came to Jesus and asked him, saying, "Who is the greatest in the kingdom of the heavens?"
Jesus called a little child to come to Him. And He put the child among them.
Jesus said, "What I am about to tell you is true. You must change and become like little children. If you do not do this, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven."
Whoever becomes humble like this child is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven.
Whoever receives a little child like this in my name receives me.
But what if someone were to tempt one of these little ones who believe in me to sin? It would be better for him to have a large millstone hung around his neck and to be drowned in the depths of the sea.
How miserable the world would be because of the things that cause people to sin! Such things would have to exist. But how miserable those who would cause them to be!
If your hand or foot causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away. Is it not better for you to enter the Kingdom of God with one hand or one foot, than to be thrown into the eternal fire of hell with both hands and both feet? In hell, the fire burns forever.
If your eye, which causes you to stumble, is good, consider it thrown from you. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of God with one eye than to have two eyes and be thrown into the hell of fire. (Matthew 18:9) **The Story of the Lost Sheep**
Beware lest you despise one of these little ones. Behold, I say to you: Their angels in heaven always see the face of my Father who is in heaven.
Translation
What's going through your mind? Imagine with me a man who has 100 sheep, and one of them gets lost. He's not going to leave 99 sheep in the mountains, is he? He's not going to go look for the one that got lost, is he?
What I am about to tell you is the truth. If that sheep finds it, it will be happier with it than with the ninety-nine that have not gone astray.
The same goes for your Father in heaven, He does not want any of these little ones to perish. When someone wrongs you
If your brother wrongs you, go and rebuke him between you and him. If he listens to you, you have gained your brother.
But what if he will not listen to you? Take one or two more, so that every matter may be established by the testimony of two or three witnesses. (Deuteronomy 19:15)
But what if he also refuses to listen to witnesses? Then report it to the church. And what if he even refuses to listen to the church? Then do not treat him as a brother. Treat him as you would treat a person who causes division or a tax collector.
What I am about to tell you is real. What you shut up on earth will be shut up in heaven. What you open on earth will be open in heaven.
Again, this is what I'm telling you. Impose your religion on the earth, agree on something that requires her, my father who is in heaven will give it to you.
"Wherever two or three are gathered in my name, there am I among them." The servant who did not have compassion.
Peter came to Jesus and asked him, "Lord, how many times must I forgive my brother when he sins against me? Up to seven times?"
Jesus answered, "I tell you, not seven times, but seventy-seven times."
The Kingdom of Heaven is like a king who wanted to collect all the money that his servants owed him.
While the king was starting to do that, a man who owed him millions of dollars was brought to him.
The man could not pay, and so his master gave an order. The man, his wife, his children, and everything he possessed had to be sold to pay his debt.
The servant fell to his knees before him. "Give me time," he pleaded. "I will return everything."
His Lord, being touched by his condition, forgave him his past, and set him free.
But then that waiter came out and found one of the other waiters to whom he owed a few dollars. He grabbed him and began to strangle him. 'Pay me what you owe me!' he said.
The other servant fell to his knees. "Give me time," he pleaded. "I will pay you back."
But the first servant refused. Instead, he went and threw the man into prison. The man will be held there until he can repay what he owes.
The remaining servants saw what happened. They were deeply affected. They went and told their master everything that happened.
Then the master called the first servant inside. He said to him, "You wicked servant, I forgave you all that you owed me because you pleaded with me.
ألم يكن عليك أن ترحم الخادم الآخر كما رحمتك أنا؟
In anger, his master handed him over to the torturers. He would be punished until he repaid everything he owed.
Thus will my Father who is in heaven treat you unless you forgive your brother from your heart.
Jesus teaches about divorce.
When Jesus had finished saying these things, he left Galilee and went into the region of Judea on the other side of the Jordan River.
Large crowds followed him. He healed them there.
Pharisees came to test him, and they asked him, "Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for every reason?"
Jesus answered, "Have you not read that He who made them at the beginning 'made them male and female,'" (Genesis 1:27)?
He said, "Therefore a man shall leave his father and his mother, and be joined to his wife, and they shall become one flesh." (Genesis 2:24)
What was two is now one. So a man should not separate what God has put together.
They asked: "So why did Moses command that a man could give his wife a divorce paper and send her away?"
Jesus answered, "Moses allowed you to divorce your wives because of the hardness of your hearts. But it was not like this from the beginning."
This is what I am telling you. Anyone who divorces his wife and marries another woman commits adultery. A man can only divorce his wife if she has been unfaithful to him.
The disciples said to him, "If that is the case between a husband and wife, it is better not to marry."
Jesus answered, "Not everyone can accept the idea of remaining single. Only those whom God has helped to live without marriage can accept that.
Some men are not able to have children like themselves who are born. Others are created this way because of other people. And others allow themselves to become this way in order to serve the Kingdom of Heaven. And whoever is able to live this way should do so." The little children are brought to him by Jesus.
Some people brought little children to Jesus. They wanted him to lay his hands on them and pray for them. But the disciples told the people to stop.
Jesus said, "Let the little children come to me and do not hinder them, for the kingdom of God belongs to such as these."
Jesus placed his hands on them. Then he left from there. Jesus and the Rich Young Man
A man came to Jesus. And he asked, "Teacher, what good thing must I do to receive eternal life?"
"Why do you ask me about what is good?" Jesus answered. "There is only one who is good. If you want to enter the kingdom, obey the commandments."
"Which?" the man asked. Jesus answered, " 'Do not murder, do not commit adultery, do not steal, do not give false testimony.' "
And warn your tribe, your kith and kin. And "Love your near neighbor as yourself."
"I have obeyed all those commandments," said the young man. "What more must I do?"
Jesus answered, "If you want to be perfect, go, sell your possessions and give to the poor. You will have treasure in heaven. Then come, follow me."
When the young man heard this, he went away sad. He was very rich.
Then Jesus said to his disciples, "Truly I say to you, it is difficult for those who are wealthy to enter the kingdom of heaven.
Again I tell you: It is difficult for a rich person to enter the kingdom of heaven. It is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle.
When the disciples heard this, they were truly amazed. They asked, "So who can be saved?"
Jesus looked at them and said, "For mortals, this is impossible, but for God, all things are possible."
Peter answered him, "We have left everything to follow you! What is the reward that will be given to us?"
"Truly I say to you, the time is coming when all that is in the tombs will come out, and the angels who are in heaven will come down with the Son of Man, and He will sit on His glorious throne. And then you also will sit upon twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Israel.
Everyone who has left houses, or brothers, or sisters, or mothers, or fathers, or fields, for my sake, will receive one hundred times more. And they will also obtain eternal life.
But many of the first will be last, and many of the last will be first.
The story of the workers in the vineyard.
The kingdom of the heavens is like a man, a king, who had a field. Early in the morning he went out to hire laborers for his vineyard.
He agreed to pay them the usual wage for a day's work. Then he sent them into his vineyard.
At about nine in the morning, he went out again. He saw others standing in the square doing nothing.
He said to them: "You two go and work in my vineyards, and I will give you what is right."
So they left. "He went out again around midday and at three and did the same thing."
At around five o'clock, he went out and found others standing around him. He asked them, "Why do you stand here all day without doing anything?"
"Because no one has hired us," they answered. He said to them, "You also go and work in my vineyard."
When evening came, the landowner spoke with the manager of the workers and said to him, "Call the workers and give them their pay. Start with the last to be hired, and then go to the first."
The newly hired workers arrived around five o'clock. Each one received their normal daily wage.
So when those who had been hired first came, they expected to receive more. But each of them also received the day's wage.
When they received it, they began to doubt its owner.
These people who were hired last only worked for one hour, they said. "You took them as us. We did most of the work and were in the hot sun all day."
The owner of the field answered one of them: "My friend, I am dealing with you fairly. Did you not agree to work for the usual day's wage?"
Take your money and leave. I wanted to give the same salary I gave you to those you hired last.
Do I not have the right to do what I want with my money? Do you feel resentful because I gave generously to others?
Therefore, those who are last will be first, and those who are first will be last." Jesus speaks again of his coming death.
Jesus was going up to Jerusalem. On the way, he took the twelve disciples aside to talk to them.
"We are going forward to Jerusalem," he said. "The Son of Man is going to be delivered over to the high priests and the scribes. He will be sentenced to death."
Then they will hand him over to the non-Jewish people. The people will mock him and flog him. They will crucify him on a piece of wood. On the third day, he will rise from the dead! "Or ask a favor from Jesus"
The mother of James and John, the sons of Zebedee, came to him with her sons. She knelt down on her knees and asked him for a favor.
"What do you want?" Jesus asked. And she said, "Promise me that one of my sons will sit at your right in your kingdom. And promise that the other will sit at your left."
"What you know, what you ask," said Jesus to them. "Can you drink the cup that I will drink?" "We can," they answered.
Jesus said to them, "You will certainly drink from my cup; but how and when this will happen, I cannot say. These places at my right and left hand are not for me to give to you. These are for those my Father has prepared for me."
The other ten students heard about this. They became angry with the two brothers.
Jesus called his disciples and said to them: "You know how the rulers of the Gentiles lord it over their people, and their great officials exercise authority over them.
Do not be like this. Instead, everyone among you who wants to be great must become your servant.
And everyone who wants to be first must be your slave.
Be like Adam. He didn't come to work with it. But he came to serve the people. He came to give his life a price to free many people. Two blind men regain their sight.
Jesus and his disciples were leaving Jericho. A great crowd followed him.
Two blind men were sitting by the roadside. When they heard that Jesus was passing by, they cried out, saying, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on us!"
The crowd bade them be silent. They bade them hold their peace. But the men cried out the more, "Master! Son of David! Have mercy on us!"
Jesus stopped and called out to them. "What do you want me to do for you?" he asked them.
"Our lord," they answered, "we want to be able to see."
Jesus felt a deep concern for them. He touched their eyes. Immediately they were able to see. And they followed him.
Jesus enters Jerusalem.
While they were all approaching Jerusalem, they came to the town of Bethphage. It was on the Mount of Olives. Jesus sent two disciples.
He said to them, "Go to the village before you. And when you enter it, you will find a donkey bound, and with it a foal. Loose them and bring them both to me."
If someone tells you something, tell them God needs them. The owner will send them in his condition.
This happened so that what was said through the Prophet might be believed. It says,
Say to the daughter of Zion, 'Behold, your king is coming to you, righteous and victorious, lowly and riding on a donkey, even on a colt, the foal of a donkey.' (Zechariah 9:9)
The disciples went and did what Jesus had commanded them.
They brought the donkey and the horse. They put their blankets for them. After that, Jesus sat on their blanket.
Large crowds spread carpets on the roads. Others cut tree branches and spread them on the roads.
Some people went ahead of him, and others followed him. They all cried out: "Hosanna to the Son of David!" "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!" (Psalm 118:26) "Hosanna in the highest!"
When Jesus entered Jerusalem, the whole city was stirred up. And the people asked, "Who is this?"
The crowd answered, "This is Jesus. He is the Prophet from Nazareth in Galilee." Jesus Cleanses the Temple
Jesus entered the temple courts. He began to drive out all who were buying and selling there. He overturned the tables of the money changers. He also overturned the benches of those who were selling doves.
He said to them, "It is written, 'The Lord has said, "My house shall be called a house of prayer for all the peoples."'" (Isaiah 56: 7) And you have made it 'a den of thieves.'" (Jeremiah 7: 11).
They came to Jesus in the temple, the blind and the lame, and he healed them there.
They saw the chief priests and the scribes doing these things, and they saw children in the temple court shouting, "Hosanna to the Son of David!" But when they saw this, they were indignant.
"Do you hear what these children are saying?" they asked him. "Yes," Jesus answered. "Have you never read in the Scriptures? It is written: 'I have made you a little lower than the heavenly beings. I have crowned you with glory and honor. I have given you dominion over all other creatures; over the birds of the air, over the beasts of the field, over all the fish in the sea, and over all the earth.' To whom will you compare me, or who is my equal?'"
Then Jesus went out from among the people and went from the city to Bethany, and remained there a night. The fig tree withers
In the early morning, Jesus was on the road returning to Jerusalem. He was hungry.
He saw a fig tree on a road. He went up to it but found nothing on it but leaves. Then he said to it: "Henceforth you shall never bear fruit!" From that time on the tree dried up.
When the disciples saw this, they were amazed and said, "How did the fig tree wither so quickly?"
Jesus answered, "Truly I tell you, if you have faith and do not doubt, not only can you do what was done to the fig tree, but also you can say to this mountain, 'Go, throw yourself into the sea,' and it will be done.
"If you believe, you will receive the things for which you ask when you pray." Jesus' authority is challenged.
Jesus entered the temple courts. As he taught there, the chief priests and the elders of the people came to him and said, "By what authority are you doing these things? And who gave you this authority?"
Jesus answered, "I will also ask you one question. If you answer me, I will tell you by what authority I do these things."
From where did John's baptism come? Did it come from heaven? Or from men?" They discussed this among themselves. They said, "If we say, 'From heaven,' he will ask us, 'Then why do you not believe him?'"
But what if we say, 'Men are'? We are afraid of people. Everyone thought that John was a prophet."
Jesus answered: "I do not know." Jesus said: "And I also will not tell you by what authority I do these things." The Parable of the Two Sons.
What are you doing in this thing? There was a man who had two sons. He went to the first son and said, "My son, go today and work in the vineyard."
"I will not be there," the boy answered. But then he changed his mind and went.
Then the father went to the other son. He told him the same thing. The son replied, "I will do it, sir." But he did not go.
Which of the two sons did what his father wanted?" "The first," they answered. Jesus said to them, "The very thing I'm about to tell you is true. Tax collectors and prostitutes will enter the Kingdom of God ahead of you.
John came to show you the right way to live. And you didn't believe him. But the tax collectors and prostitutes believed him. You saw this. But even then you didn't turn away from your sins and believe him. The story of the tenants.
Here is another story. A man owned a piece of land, so he planted a vineyard in it. He surrounded it with a fence, dug a wine press in it, and also built a watchtower. He rented the vineyard to farmers, then traveled.
When the harvest was near, he sent his servant to the tenants. And he told the servants to gather his share of the fruit.
But the tenants seized his servants. They beat one of them. They killed another. And they threw stones at a third, intending to kill him.
Then the man sent other servants to the tenants. He sent more than he had the first time. The tenants treated the servants the same way.
In the end, he sent his son to them. "My son will be respected," he said.
But the tenants saw the boy coming. They said to one another, "This is the one who is going to take possession of all the owner's property the day he dies. Let's go kill him. And then everything will be ours."
And they took him and brought him out of the vineyard and killed him.
When the owner of the vineyard returns, what will he do with these tenants?
"They will destroy these bastards," he replied. "Afterwards, he will rent the league to others. They will give him his share of the harvest at harvest time."
Jesus said to them, "Have you not read in the Scriptures: 'The stone which the builders rejected has become the chief cornerstone.' This was from the Lord, and it is amazing in our eyes?'" (Psalm 118: 22-23)
So here is what I say to you: The kingdom of God will be taken away from you and given to a people who will produce its fruit.
Anyone who falls on that stone will be broken to pieces. But the stone will crush anyone it falls on.
The chief priests and Sadducees heard about Jesus's deeds. They knew that he was speaking about them.
They searched for a way to arrest him. But they were afraid of the crowd. The people believed that Jesus was a prophet.
The Wedding Dinner Story
Jesus said to them other many parables, his all words were parables.
Here is like the kingdom of heaven. Like a king preparing a wedding feast for his son.
He sent his servants to those invited to the dinner. The servants instructed them to come. But they refused.
Then he sent some other servants. "Tell the invited guests," he said, "that I have prepared my dinner. I have killed my calves and my fattened animals. Everything is ready. Come to the wedding dinner."
But people did not pay attention. One went to catch up on his vineyard. And another went to catch up on his business.
The rest took his servants. They tortured and killed them.
The king was filled with rage. He sent his army to destroy them. They killed those murderers and burned their city.
Then the king said to his servant, "The wedding dinner is ready. But those I invited were not fit to attend."
Go to the alleys. Let dinner be mine, whoever you find.
The servants went out into the streets. They gathered all the people they found, good and bad. Soon the wedding hall was filled with guests.
The king went in to see the guests, and he saw a man who was not dressed in wedding clothes.
"Friend," he asked her, "how did you get in here without the wedding clothes?" The man couldn't think of anything to say.
Then the king said to his servant, "Bind his hands and feet. Throw him out into the darkness. There people will weep and grind their teeth."
Many are called, but few are chosen." Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar?
Therefore the Pharisees went out and took counsel against him, how they might entangle him in his talk.
They sent their followers to him, along with the Herodians. They said to him, "Teacher, we know that you are a man of integrity. You teach the way of God in truth. You do not permit people to direct you or to tell you what to say. You do not give attention to people's importance."
So tell us then, what do you think? Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar or not?
But Jesus knew their wicked schemes. He said, "You wolves in sheep's clothing! Why are you trying to trap me?"
Show me the coin used by people to pay taxes. Bring him a silver coin.
Ask them: "Whose picture is this? And whose words are these?"
"Caesar," they said. Then he said to them, "Give to Caesar what is Caesar's. And give to God what is God's."
When they heard this, they were astonished. So they left him and went away. Marriage, when the dead rise.
On that same day, the Sadducees came to Jesus with a question. They do not believe that people rise from the dead.
"Teacher," they said, "this is what Moses told us. If a man dies without having children, his brother must marry the widow. He must have children to carry on his brother's name."
There were seven of us brothers. The first one got married and died. Since he didn't have any children, his wife left her brother.
The same thing happened to the second and third brothers, it even happened to the seventh brother.
In the end, the woman died.
Now, when the dead rise, to whom will she be wife? All seven were married to her."
Jesus answered, "You are wrong, because you do not know the Scriptures, nor the power of God."
When the dead rise, they will not marry. Nor will their fathers give them away in marriage. They will be like the angels in heaven.
What about the dead who rise from the graves? Have you not read what God has said to you?
He said, "I am the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob." (Exodus 3:6) "He is not the God of the dead, but the God of the living."
When the crowds heard this, they were astonished at his teaching. The most important commandment is
When the Pharisees heard that the Sadducees had not been able to answer Jesus, they assembled.
One of them was a scholar, so he tried to test Jesus with a question.
"Professor," he asked, "which is the most important commandment in the law?"
Jesus answered, "You shall love the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul and with all your mind." (Deuteronomy 6: 5)
This is the first and most important commandment.
The second example is like the first. 'Love your neighbor as yourself.' (Leviticus 19:18)
All that is written in the Law and the Prophets is based on these two commandments." Who is the Son of Christ?
The Pharisees gathered together. And Jesus asked them,
"What do you all believe about the Messiah? Where was he born?" "Born of David," they answered.
He said to them, "Why then does David call him Lord? For the Holy Spirit spoke through David's own mouth:
The Lord said to my Lord, "Sit at my right hand until I make your enemies your footstool." (Psalm 110:1)
If David called him "sir," how could he be his son?
No one was able to get a word in edgewise with him. From that day on, no one dared to ask him even a single question.
Jesus put the Pharisees and teachers of the law on trial.
Jesus spoke to the crowds and to his disciples.
The teachers of the law and the Pharisees sit in the seat of Moses, he said.
So you have to obey them. Do everything they tell you. But don't do like they do. They don't do what they say.
They pile heavy burdens on the shoulders of others, but they themselves do not wish to move a single finger to move them.
All they do they do to be seen by men. They carry on their foreheads and their arms small boxes containing verses from the Holy Book. They make the boxes very broad. And they make the ribbons on their coats very long.
They love to sit in the place of honor at dinners. They also love to have the most important seats in temples.
They like to be greeted in the markets. And they like people to call them "sir".
But you should not be called 'my lord.' You have one master and you are all brothers.
Do not say to anyone on earth: 'My father,' for your father is the only one in heaven.
You should not be called "Teacher." You have one Teacher, and that is Christ.
The most important person among you will be your servant.
He who raises his head will bring it down, and he who brings it down will raise it.
What a terrible thing awaits you, you teachers and Pharisees, you impostors! You lock the door of the kingdom of heaven in people's faces. And you yourselves do not enter. And you will not let those who try to enter do so.
Translation
Oh, what a terrible thing for you, you teachers and Pharisees! You impostors! You travel everywhere to make a single convert to your religion. Then you make him twice as much a child of hell as you yourselves are.
Oh, what a blind people you are, and how great is your error! You say, "If anyone swears by the Temple, it is nothing; but whoever swears by the gold of the Temple, he is bound by his oath."
You are blind and foolish! Which is more important? Is it the gold? Or is it the temple which makes the gold sacred?
As it says without a doubt, "If one swears by the name of the altar, his oath shall not be invalidated. But if he swears by the name of the sacrifice offered on it, he shall be bound to keep his oath."
Oh, you men! Blind! What is the most important thing? Is it the gift? Or the altar that makes the gift sacred?
Whoever swears by an altar, he is swearing by it and by everything on it.
And whoever swears by the temple, he is swearing by it and by everyone in it.
And whoever swears by the heaven has indeed sworn by the throne of Allah and by whoever is on it that verily it is the truth and whatever is between them.
Oh, how severe it is for you, you legal experts and fraudulent Pharisees, you hypocrites! You give God a tenth of the mint, the dill, and the cumin, but you have neglected the weightier matters of the law: justice, mercy, and faithfulness. You ought to have practiced the more important matters first.
O ye blind guides! You strain out a gnat from your drink, but you swallow a camel whole!
Woe to you, teachers of the law and you, Pharisees, you hypocrites! You clean the outside of the cup and of the plate, but inside of you, you are full of greed. You want only to satisfy your desires.
You blind Pharisee! First clean the inside of the cup and dish, and then the outside will also be clean.
Woe to you, teachers of the law and you, Pharisees, hypocrites! For you are like whitewashed tombs, which look beautiful on the outside but on the inside are full of the bones of the dead and of all kinds of filth and detestable things.
The same goes for you. From the outside, it looks like you're doing what's right. But on the inside, you're full of what's wrong. You pretend to be what you're not.
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! You build the tombs of the prophets and decorate the shrines of the righteous.
And she says, "If we had lived in the days of those who lived before us, we would not have done what they did. We would not have helped in the killing of the prophets."
So you bear witness against yourselves, you admit that you are the sons of those who killed the prophets.
Well, complete the sins that others who lived before you started for you!
"O nest of poisonous vipers! How will you escape being sentenced to the fire?"
Therefore, I will send you prophets, wise men, and teachers. Some of them you will kill, others you will crucify, and still others you will flog in your synagogues and persecute from city to city.
So you will pay the price for all the righteous blood that was shed on earth. I mean, from the righteous blood of Abel to the blood of Zechariah son of Berekiah. Zechariah whom you killed between the temple and the altar.
What I will tell you is true. All of this will happen to those who are alive now.
Jerusalem! Jerusalem! You who kill the prophets and stone those sent to you. How often I have wanted to gather your children together, as a hen gathers her brood under her wings. But you would not let me!
Look, your house is empty.
I tell you, you will not see me until you say: "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord." (Psalm 118: 26)
End Times Signs
Jesus left the temple. He was walking away when his disciples came to him. They wanted to call his attention to the temple buildings.
"Do you see all these things?" Jesus asked. "Truly I tell you, not one stone will be left here upon another; every stone will be thrown down."
Jesus was sitting on the Mount of Olives. And there came to Him the disciples privately, saying, "Tell us, when shall these things be? And what shall be the sign of Your coming, and of the end of the world?"
Jesus answered, "Beware! Be vigilant, lest someone deceive you.
Many will come in my name, saying, 'I am the Messiah!' And they will lead many astray.
You will hear about wars. You will hear people talking about wars of the future. Do not be afraid. This has to happen. But the end is not yet.
Nation will rise against nation, and kingdom against kingdom. There will be great earthquakes, famines and pestilences in many places.
This is all just the beginning of labor pains.
Then they will hand you over to be tortured and killed, and all the nations will hate you for my name's sake.
In those days, many will fall away from the faith. They will betray one another and hate one another. Many false prophets will appear and deceive many people. Because of the increase of wickedness, the love of many will grow cold.
Many false prophecies will appear. They will deceive many people.
Because of the spread of evil, the love of most people will grow cold.
But whoever endures until the end will be saved.
This good news of the kingdom will be proclaimed throughout the entire world. It will serve as a testimony for all the nations. Then, the end will come.
