Jesus rises from the dead.
It was the end of the Sabbath. It was the dawn of the first day of the week. Mary Magdalene and the other Mary went to check on the tomb.
There was a strong earthquake. A king descended from God from the sky. The king went to the grave. He moved the stone and sat on it.
His body shines like lightning. His clothes are white as snow.
The guards were so afraid of him that they trembled and became like dead men.
The angel said to them, "Do not be afraid. I know that you are looking for Jesus who was crucified."
He's not here! He's risen, just as He said He would rise! Come and see the place where He was lying.
Walk quickly! Say to his disciples, "He has been raised from the dead. He is going before you to Galilee. There you will see him." And now, I have told you.
The women quickly came out of the tomb, trembling with fear and great joy, and ran to tell His disciples.
Suddenly, Jesus met them. "Peace be upon you!" he said. They came to him, seized his feet and worshipped him.
Then Jesus said to them, "Do not be afraid. Go and tell my brothers to go to Galilee. There they will see me." The guards' report to the chief priests.
And here are some women who had come from the grave, telling the disciples, and when the guards heard, they went to the elders, telling them all that had been.
When the chief priests met with the elders, they made a plan. They gave a large sum of money to the soldiers.
They told the soldiers: "We want you to say: 'His disciples came by night and stole his body while we were sleeping.'"
If the governor hears this report, we will give him a bribe. This will keep you out of trouble.
The soldiers took the money and did as they were commanded. This story spread among the Jews to this day. The last commands of Jesus to his disciples
Then the disciples of the eleven apostles went to Galilee. They went to the mountain to which Jesus had commanded them to go.
When they saw him, they bowed down to him. But some still had their doubts.
Then Jesus came to them and said, "All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me."
Go therefore and make disciples of all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit.
Teach them to obey everything I have commanded you. And be sure that I am with you always, to the end."
John the Baptist is preparing the way.
This is the beginning of the gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God.
A long time ago, the prophet Isaiah wrote, saying: "I will send my messenger, who will prepare the way before you," my messenger." (Malachi 3: 1)
"The messenger calls in the desert, 'You who thirst for the Lord, come and drink deeply from His limitless spring.' " (Isaiah 40: 3)
And so John came. He was baptizing people in the desert. He also preached that people should be baptized and repent of their sins. Then God would forgive them.
All the people from every part of the countryside of Judea, and all the inhabitants of Jerusalem, went out to him. They confessed their sins, and he baptized them in the river Jordan.
John wore clothes made of camel hair. He wore a belt of leather around his waist. He ate locusts and wild honey.
This is what John used to preach: "After me comes one who is mightier than I, whose sandals I am not worthy to untie."
I baptize you with water; but he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit." The Baptism and Temptation of Jesus
In those days Jesus came from Nazareth of Galilee and was baptized by John in the Jordan River.
As Jesus was coming up out of the water, at that moment he saw the heavens being torn open and the Spirit descending on him like a dove.
A voice called to him from heaven, saying, "You are my son, whom I love; I am very pleased with you."
And when the days of his temptation were completed, the Holy Ghost carried him away into the wilderness.
Jesus was in the desert for 40 days. There, the devil tempted him. Wild animals did not harm Jesus. Angels took care of him. Jesus chooses the first disciples.
After John was imprisoned, Jesus went to Hejaz. He was preaching the word of God.
The time has come, he said. The kingdom of God is near. Turn away from your sins and believe the good news!
Once Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee. There he saw Simon and his brother Andrew. They were throwing a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
"Come, follow Me," said Jesus. "I will make you fish for people."
They scarcely began to hear it when they abandoned their nets and followed him.
Then Jesus walked a little farther. And while he was walking, he saw Simon Peter and his brother Andrew. They were in a boat, preparing their nets.
Jesus exorcises an evil spirit.
Jesus and those with Him went to Capernaum. When the Sabbath came, He entered the synagogue and began to teach.
The people were amazed at his teaching, because he taught them as one having authority, and not as the scribes.
At that moment, a man cried out in their synagogue. He was controlled by an evil spirit. He said,
"What do you want with us, Jesus of Nazareth? Have you come to destroy us? I know who you are. You are the holy one of God!"
"Be quiet!" Jesus said firmly. "Come out of him!"
The evil spirit violently shook the man. Then it came out of him with a cry.
Everyone was astonished, and they began to question one another, saying, "What is this? A new teaching! And with such authority! He even commands evil spirits, and they obey him."
News of Jesus spread quickly throughout Galilee. Jesus heals many people.
Jesus and those with him went out of the mosque. Immediately they went along with James and John to the house of Simon and Andrew.
Simon's mother-in-law was lying sick in bed, suffering from a fever. They told Jesus about her.
He went to her, took her hands and helped her rise. The fever had broken. Afterwards, she began to work for them.
That night, after sunset, the people brought to Jesus all the sick. They also brought all who were possessed by demons.
All the people in the city gathered at the gate.
Jesus healed many of them, and they had all kinds of diseases. He also cast out many demons, but he did not allow the demons to speak, because they knew who he was. Jesus prays in a quiet place.
It was very early in the morning, and still dark. Jesus got up and left the house. He went to a place where he could be alone. There he prayed.
Simon and his friends went to look for Jesus.
When they found him, they cried out, "Everyone is looking for you!"
Jesus answered, "Let us go to some other place. I want to go to the nearby villages. I must preach there also. For this I have come."
Jesus entered Galilee, in all of Galilee. In order to preach in their synagogues. And in order to cast out demons. Jesus saw a man who had leprosy.
A man who was afflicted with a skin disease came to Jesus. He knelt down before Jesus and begged Him, saying, "If You are willing, You can make me clean."
Jesus was filled with deep concern. He reached out and touched the man. "I am willing to do this," he said. "Be 'clean'!"
Immediately the disease left him. He was cured.
Jesus immediately sent him away. The man was given a strong warning.
"What you're saying about this to someone," he said. "Go to the priest and confess to him. Make the sacrifices that Moses commanded. This will be an argument against the priest and the helpers that you are 'pure' and 'clean'."
But the man went out and began to spread the news everywhere. So Jesus could no longer enter a town openly. He stayed outside in lonely places, but people came to him from everywhere.
Jesus heals a man who could not walk.
After a few days, Jesus entered Capernaum again. The people heard that he had returned home.
A large crowd of people gathered until there was no more room, not even outside the door. And Jesus taught them.
Four of those who came were carrying a man who could not walk.
But they were unable to get him to Jesus because of the crowd. That is why they went up to the roof of the house where Jesus was. Then they lowered the man on his stretcher through the roof.
Jesus saw their faith. He said to the man, "Son, your sins are forgiven."
Some jurists used to sit there. They used to think,
Why does this gentleman speak in this manner? He is saying something extremely malicious! Only God is capable of forgiving sins!
Jesus immediately knew what they were thinking. That's why he said to them, "Why are you thinking about this?"
Is it easier to say to this man, "Your sins are forgiven"? Or to say, "Get up, take your mat and walk"?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." Then Jesus spoke to the man who could not walk.
He told me, "Let me tell you, get up. Take your stuff and go home."
The man got up and took his mat. After that, he went away, and all the people were looking at him. All the people there were astounded. They praised God and said, "We have never seen anything like this in our lives!" Jesus chose Levi.
Again Jesus went out beside the Sea of Galilee. A great crowd came to him. He began to teach them.
As he was walking, he saw Levi son of Alphaeus sitting at the tax office. And Jesus said to him, "Follow me." And he rose and followed him.
Later, Jesus was having dinner at the house of Levi. Many tax collectors and "sinners" were eating with him and his disciples. They were part of the large crowd that was following Jesus.
There were some Pharisees who were teachers of the law. They saw Jesus eating with "sinners" and tax collectors. So they asked his disciples, "Why does he eat with tax collectors and 'sinners'?"
Jesus heard that, so he said to them: "Those who are in good health do not need a doctor. It is the sick who need one. I did not come to call the righteous, but sinners to follow me." Jesus is asked about fasting.
The disciples of John and the Pharisees were fasting. People came to Jesus. They said to him, "The disciples of John fast, and the disciples of the Pharisees fast. But your disciples do not fast. Why do your disciples not fast?"
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom fast while he is with them? As long as the bridegroom is with them, they cannot fast.
But the time will come when the bridegroom will be taken from them. On that day they will go away without eating.
People do not patch a piece of new cloth onto old clothing. If they did, the new piece would be pulled away from the old. It would make that tear even worse.
People don't pour new wine into old wineskins. If they do, the wine will burst the wineskins. And then the wine and the wineskins will both be ruined. No, everything should be poured into new wineskins." Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath.
On a Saturday, Jesus was walking with his disciples in the wheat fields. So the disciples began to pick the heads of wheat.
The Pharisees said to Jesus, "Look! Doing this on the Sabbath is a violation of the law. Why do your disciples do this?"
He answered, "Have you never read about what David did? He and his men were hungry. They were in need of food."
This was in the time of Abiathar the high priest. David entered the house of God and ate the bread of presentation, which was not lawful for him to eat, but only for the priests. And David also gave of this bread to his men.
Then Jesus said to them, "The Sabbath was made for man's sake, and not man for the Sabbath's sake."
So the Son of Man is Lord even of the Sabbath."
The cure is on Saturday.
Again Jesus entered the synagogue. There was a man there who had a withered hand.
Some Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. They were watching him closely. They wanted to see if he would heal the man on the Sabbath.
Jesus spoke to the man who had a withered and shriveled hand. "Come forward," he said.
Then Jesus asked them, "What does the Law say we must do on the Sabbath? Is it to do good or to do evil? Is it to save life or to destroy it?" But no one answered him.
Jesus looked at them angrily, for He was very disturbed because their hearts were hardened. Then He said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." And he stretched it out, and his hand was restored like the hand of a normal person.
Then the Pharisees went out and began to plot with the Herodians against Jesus, seeking to kill him. The crowds follow Jesus.
Jesus walked to the Sea of Galilee with his disciples, and a large crowd from Galilee followed him.
The people heard about all the things Jesus was doing. And many people came to him, not only from Judea and Jerusalem, but also from Idumea, and from beyond the Jordan, and from the district of Tyre and Sidon.
Because of the crowd, Jesus instructed his disciples to prepare a small boat for him. This way the people would not be able to crowd around him.
Jesus was healing many people. Therefore, the sick were being pushed forward to touch him.
When the people who had evil spirits saw him, they fell down in front of him. The spirits cried out, saying, "You are the Son of God!"
But Jesus commanded them not to tell who he was. Jesus appoints the twelve apostles.
Jesus went up a mountain. He called some people to come to him, and they came.
He appointed twelve of them and named them apostles. From that time on, they were with him, and he also sent them out to preach.
They will have authority to cast out demons.
Jesus appointed the twelve; and among them Simon whom he named Peter.
There was James the son of Zebedee and his brother John. Jesus gave them the name "Boanerges". Boanerges means "sons of thunder".
There were also Andrew, Philip, Bartholomew, Matthew, Thomas, and James the son of Alphaeus. And there were Thaddaeus and Simon the Zealot.
Judas Iscariot was one of them, he is also. He is the one who will deliver Jesus to his enemies later. Jesus and Beelzebub
Jesus entered a house. Once again, large crowds gathered, so large that Jesus and his disciples couldn't even eat.
His family heard about this, which led them to go and take him in his condition. They said, "This man is mentally ill."
There was a group from the Law who came from Jerusalem. They said, "He is possessed by Beelzebul, and he drives out demons by the power of the chief of demons."
Jesus called the multitudes and He recited to them these parables, saying, "How can Satan cast out Satan?"
If a kingdom fights against itself, it cannot stand.
If a family splits, it cannot endure.
